1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,415
Can you show me secrets in Git?

2
00:00:07,967 --> 00:00:14,056
Why a password, API token or access key in a Git repository is a serious incident:

3
00:00:14,056 --> 00:00:18,288
Git remembers every version, every clone has a full copy,

4
00:00:18,288 --> 00:00:22,521
and deleting the file in a new commit does not remove it.

5
00:00:22,521 --> 00:00:29,946
We commit a (fake) secret, try to "delete" it, find it again, and set up the habits that prevent it.

6
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:36,100
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

7
00:00:36,633 --> 00:00:43,519
Ada adds the app configuration, secrets included (all values here are fake), and pushes:

8
00:00:43,519 --> 00:00:45,788
She notices and "removes" it:

9
00:00:46,333 --> 00:00:48,537
She notices and "removes" it:

10
00:00:48,537 --> 00:00:53,097
Someone else clones the repository later (a new clone, eve).

11
00:00:53,097 --> 00:00:57,353
The file is not in the folder, but it is in the history:

12
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:02,587
Someone else clones the repository later (a new clone, eve).

13
00:01:02,587 --> 00:01:06,961
The file is not in the folder, but it is in the history:

14
00:01:06,961 --> 00:01:11,570
Anyone with read access, now or in the future, can do this.

15
00:01:13,333 --> 00:01:14,963
Let me try something.

16
00:01:14,963 --> 00:01:19,386
Believing git rm was enough, nobody rotates the password,

17
00:01:19,386 --> 00:01:24,663
and the repository is made public / forked / cloned by a contractor.

18
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:27,362
Ask, in this order:

19
00:01:27,362 --> 00:01:27,589
1.

20
00:01:27,589 --> 00:01:29,182
Was it pushed?

21
00:01:29,182 --> 00:01:30,434
Not pushed:

22
00:01:30,434 --> 00:01:36,692
amend/reset locally (lesson 83's fix) and you are done.

23
00:01:36,692 --> 00:01:36,919
2.

24
00:01:38,667 --> 00:01:42,088
The repository side of the fix, after rotation:

25
00:01:42,088 --> 00:01:48,131
keep configuration with secrets out of Git for good, and commit a template instead.

26
00:01:49,867 --> 00:01:50,580
Got it.

27
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:53,331
What do I need to remember?

28
00:01:53,867 --> 00:01:58,846
Deleting a secret in a new commit does not remove it from history or from clones.

29
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:02,019
A pushed secret is compromised:

30
00:02:02,019 --> 00:02:05,060
rotate it first, then clean history.

31
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,383
Prevent:

32
00:02:06,383 --> 00:02:14,115
.gitignore, .env.example, secret managers, pre-commit and server-side scanning.

33
00:02:15,867 --> 00:02:19,617
I keep hearing about removing sensitive data.

34
00:02:19,617 --> 00:02:21,201
What is it, really?

35
00:02:21,767 --> 00:02:27,673
How to rewrite a repository's history so that a file (or a string) never existed,

36
00:02:27,673 --> 00:02:33,288
with git filter-repo, and what else has to happen for the cleanup to be real:

37
00:02:33,288 --> 00:02:38,101
force-pushing, every clone being replaced, and server-side caches.

38
00:02:38,101 --> 00:02:42,330
The credential itself must already be rotated (lesson 89):

39
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:45,611
history rewriting is cleanup, not protection.

40
00:02:47,367 --> 00:02:51,766
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

41
00:02:52,300 --> 00:02:59,964
Work on a fresh mirror clone (all branches and tags, nothing else), as filter-repo recommends:

42
00:02:59,964 --> 00:03:02,410
Remove .env from every commit:

43
00:03:02,967 --> 00:03:05,206
Remove .env from every commit:

44
00:03:05,206 --> 00:03:11,776
Every commit from "Add configuration" onwards has a new ID, and no commit contains .env.

45
00:03:11,776 --> 00:03:18,121
Push the new history over the old one (a mirror push includes all branches and tags):

46
00:03:18,667 --> 00:03:24,900
Every commit from "Add configuration" onwards has a new ID, and no commit contains .env.

47
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:30,921
Push the new history over the old one (a mirror push includes all branches and tags):

48
00:03:30,921 --> 00:03:34,816
(filter-repo removes the origin remote after rewriting,

49
00:03:34,816 --> 00:03:39,066
to prevent an accidental push; push to the URL explicitly.).

50
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:42,607
Let me try something.

51
00:03:42,607 --> 00:03:45,360
Grace still has the old history.

52
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:49,859
The old commits, .env included, are back on the server:

53
00:03:49,859 --> 00:03:57,345
Grace's merge connected her old history (which still contains "Add configuration") to the rewritten one,

54
00:03:57,345 --> 00:03:59,145
and her push uploaded it.

55
00:03:59,145 --> 00:04:03,680
A rewrite only works if every copy switches to the new history.

56
00:04:05,433 --> 00:04:12,788
Rewrite the server again, then Grace replaces her clone instead of merging (keeping her new work as a patch):

57
00:04:14,533 --> 00:04:15,247
Got it.

58
00:04:15,247 --> 00:04:17,998
What do I need to remember?

59
00:04:18,533 --> 00:04:22,028
Rotate first; rewrite history second.

60
00:04:22,567 --> 00:04:30,566
git filter-repo --invert-paths --path FILE (or --replace-text) on a mirror clone, then force-push everything.

61
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:37,110
Every clone must be replaced; otherwise the old history comes back with the next push.

62
00:04:38,867 --> 00:04:41,276
How does commit signing work?

63
00:04:41,833 --> 00:04:46,841
Anyone can write any name and email into a commit (lesson 04:

64
00:04:46,841 --> 00:04:48,976
it is just configuration).

65
00:04:48,976 --> 00:04:55,133
A signature proves that the commit was made by the holder of a private key.

66
00:04:55,133 --> 00:05:03,918
We sign commits and tags with an SSH key (simplest, Git 2.34+), verify them, and see how GPG does the same.

67
00:05:05,667 --> 00:05:10,066
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

68
00:05:10,600 --> 00:05:14,104
Configure Git to sign with that SSH key:

69
00:05:14,667 --> 00:05:16,806
Sign a commit with -S:

70
00:05:17,367 --> 00:05:20,451
The signature is part of the commit object.

71
00:05:20,451 --> 00:05:25,113
To verify, Git needs to know which keys to trust for which email:

72
00:05:25,113 --> 00:05:26,906
the allowed signers file.

73
00:05:28,667 --> 00:05:30,297
Let me try something.

74
00:05:30,297 --> 00:05:35,421
A teammate clones the repository and verifies Ada's latest commit:

75
00:05:35,967 --> 00:05:42,351
gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile needs to be configured and exist for ssh signature verification:

76
00:05:42,351 --> 00:05:49,087
a signature alone proves nothing until you decide whose public key you trust for which identity.

77
00:05:49,087 --> 00:05:59,471
For SSH signing that decision is the allowed signers file; for GPG it is your keyring and its trust settings; on GitHub it is the keys users upload.

78
00:06:01,233 --> 00:06:08,533
Teams commit an allowed signers file to the repository (reviewed like code), and each clone points Git at it:

79
00:06:10,267 --> 00:06:10,980
Got it.

80
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:13,731
What do I need to remember?

81
00:06:14,267 --> 00:06:19,011
Author fields are claims; signatures are proof of key possession.

82
00:06:19,567 --> 00:06:20,845
SSH signing:

83
00:06:20,845 --> 00:06:29,901
gpg.format ssh, user.signingkey, commit.gpgsign; verify with an allowed signers file.

84
00:06:30,467 --> 00:06:37,041
Upload the signing key to GitHub for "Verified", and require signed commits on protected branches.

85
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:41,719
Why would I need supply chain security?

86
00:06:42,267 --> 00:06:46,712
Your software is built from repositories you do not control:

87
00:06:46,712 --> 00:06:50,565
libraries, Helm charts, GitHub Actions, base images.

88
00:06:50,565 --> 00:06:52,047
The Git-level risks:

89
00:06:52,047 --> 00:06:57,086
mutable references (a tag or branch can be moved to malicious code),

90
00:06:57,086 --> 00:07:03,310
spoofed authors, compromised accounts and malicious commits hidden in large changes.

91
00:07:03,310 --> 00:07:07,311
We exploit a moved tag locally, then pin by commit ID.

92
00:07:09,067 --> 00:07:13,466
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

93
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,545
Pinning by tag trusts that the tag never moves.

94
00:07:17,545 --> 00:07:20,863
An attacker who gained push access moves it:

95
00:07:20,863 --> 00:07:26,519
The next build that fetches v1.0.0 runs different code under the same name:

96
00:07:27,067 --> 00:07:33,236
The next build that fetches v1.0.0 runs different code under the same name:

97
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,942
Let me try something.

98
00:07:36,942 --> 00:07:39,439
Authorship is not identity.

99
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,672
git log happily shows "Grace Hopper":

100
00:07:42,672 --> 00:07:44,694
author fields are free text.

101
00:07:44,694 --> 00:07:50,182
On GitHub, the commit would even show Grace's avatar if her email is public.

102
00:07:50,182 --> 00:07:55,309
The only Git-level evidence is the signature, and this commit has none:

103
00:07:57,067 --> 00:08:02,741
Make unsigned or unverified commits stand out, and refuse them where it matters:

104
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:05,213
Got it.

105
00:08:05,213 --> 00:08:07,964
What do I need to remember?

106
00:08:08,500 --> 00:08:12,597
Tags and branches are mutable; commit IDs are not:

107
00:08:12,597 --> 00:08:15,054
pin dependencies by commit ID.

108
00:08:15,600 --> 00:08:22,324
Author names are claims; trust signatures from known keys, reviews and protected branches.

109
00:08:22,867 --> 00:08:28,406
Least-privilege tokens and 2FA limit what a compromised account or action can do.
